86Box's FIFO thread runs its own FIFO and the command ring in the order the
guest wrote them (doc 21 §13, M14).

The guest has one bus into the chip: a write it puts in the memory FIFO
(an LFB or texture write, `voodoo->fifo`) and a word it puts in the command
ring reach the pixel pipeline in the order it made them. 86Box's thread had
no way to say which came first -- it drained the whole memory FIFO, then ran
the ring until it was empty -- so with the ring running behind, which in a
game it always is (Carmageddon's race: ~20,000 words), an LFB write landed
far from where the guest put it. Both ways round, and both were seen:

  * draining the memory FIFO first put a HUD written through the LFB *before*
    the world geometry the guest had already queued, and the world painted
    over it. The user's run of Carmageddon's 3dfx build: the HUD almost never
    appeared.
  * finishing the ring first put that HUD *after* the swap that followed it,
    so it landed in the buffer the swap had just turned into the back one and
    showed a frame late or not at all. The same game before this patch
    existed: the HUD flashed in and out.

So each entry now carries the ring's write pointer as it was queued
(`cmdfifo_mark`, in the three pad bytes' place). It runs once the ring has
been consumed that far and not before, and the ring loop yields the moment
the memory FIFO's head is due. Signed difference, because both counters run
on for ever; with the command FIFO off the marks equal the read pointer and
nothing changes. The per-type runs inside the memory FIFO stop at an entry
the ring has not reached yet, which is the one-line change repeated five
times.

It costs nothing: no wait anywhere, the same work in the guest's order. The
vCPU waited for the memory FIFO instead for a day, and Carmageddon priced
that at 3,200 waits and 1.8 s of vCPU time per 5 s.

The files are 86Box's, vendored verbatim in voodoo/86box and overlaid into
hw/voodoo by prepare-qemu.sh, which is why this is a patch on the overlay
rather than an edit there. Drop it when upstream 86Box has the fix and
scripts/sync-86box-voodoo.sh brings it in.

A mark the ring can never reach -- which only a reset of its counters under
an entry already queued can make, at a grSstWinClose or a fresh
sst1InitRegisters -- is stale rather than in the future, and is taken as
due: waiting for it would strand the entry and then fill this FIFO under
the next guest write. `voodoo/voodoo2.c` also runs both out before it lets
such a write through, so in practice there is nothing queued to strand.

A mark the ring can never reach -- which only a reset of its counters under
an entry already queued can make, at a grSstWinClose or a fresh
sst1InitRegisters -- is stale rather than in the future, and is taken as
due: waiting for it would strand the entry and then fill this FIFO under
the next guest write. It is the only thing that has to hold across a
grSstWinClose or a fresh sst1InitRegisters, and it holds without anyone
waiting -- draining this FIFO from the guest's side instead would mean
holding 86Box's `flush` while a swap goes past, which flips the buffer
where it stands rather than at the retrace, and tears the frame.

